WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.200
<v Narrator>Welcome.

2
00:00:01.350 --> 00:00:07.350
Think about the last time you had a question at work and held it back.

3
00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:10.300
Maybe you worried you would look unprepared.

4
00:00:10.450 --> 00:00:16.450
Maybe the person you would be questioning had more experience, or more say over your

5
00:00:16.600 --> 00:00:17.800
next assignment.

6
00:00:17.950 --> 00:00:22.350
Maybe the meeting was moving fast, and nobody else seemed confused.

7
00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:24.100
Holding back felt reasonable.

8
00:00:24.250 --> 00:00:26.250
Most people have done it.

9
00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:32.000
This course is about that moment, and about the moment right after someone does

10
00:00:32.150 --> 00:00:33.350
speak up.

11
00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:38.700
Psychological safety is about what people expect will happen when they ask a

12
00:00:38.850 --> 00:00:42.450
question, admit a mistake, raise a concern or disagree.

13
00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:45.800
It is not about being nice or comfortable.

14
00:00:45.950 --> 00:00:49.950
It is about whether useful information can reach the work.

15
00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:57.300
You will look at what the idea means, why the risk is not the same for everyone, and

16
00:00:57.450 --> 00:01:00.250
how to raise and receive a question.

17
00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:06.000
Then you will step into a workplace simulation where your choices shape what happens

18
00:01:06.150 --> 00:01:07.350
next.

19
00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:13.300
<v Narrator>Here is a small exchange from one of the readings behind this course.

20
00:01:13.450 --> 00:01:18.650
A colleague asks, “Could this instruction leave someone without a usable route?” The

21
00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:22.800
reply is, “We have already discussed that.” Nothing dramatic happened.

22
00:01:22.950 --> 00:01:25.750
Yet look at what the reply does.

23
00:01:25.900 --> 00:01:28.300
It says a discussion took place.

24
00:01:28.450 --> 00:01:31.650
It does not say what that discussion settled.

25
00:01:31.800 --> 00:01:36.600
The colleague may need a reference, a correction or a clearer answer.

26
00:01:36.750 --> 00:01:39.550
Instead, the reply mostly discourages asking again.

27
00:01:39.700 --> 00:01:44.500
If questions keep getting replies like that, people may stop offering the

28
00:01:44.650 --> 00:01:46.250
uncertainty the work needs.

29
00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:48.400
The reading is careful here.

30
00:01:48.550 --> 00:01:52.950
That is a possible pattern, not proof of how everyone feels.

31
00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:58.300
So start with the specific response, and the work question it leaves open.

32
00:01:59.050 --> 00:02:01.850
<v Narrator>Why does this matter in public service?

33
00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:06.800
Amy Edmondson, a professor at Harvard Business School, is the researcher most

34
00:02:06.950 --> 00:02:09.350
associated with the term psychological safety.

35
00:02:09.500 --> 00:02:14.300
In her hospital research, the better teams reported more errors, not fewer.

36
00:02:14.450 --> 00:02:17.250
They felt able to talk about them.

37
00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:21.400
Her work also argues that fear is a poor motivator.

38
00:02:21.550 --> 00:02:27.150
It narrows thinking, and it leads people to hold back information that leaders need.

39
00:02:27.300 --> 00:02:32.500
Many major failures, in companies and in public organizations, trace back to people

40
00:02:32.650 --> 00:02:35.450
who saw a problem and stayed silent.

41
00:02:35.600 --> 00:02:38.400
The reading points to human services directly.

42
00:02:38.550 --> 00:02:44.150
A case review or an incident debrief that brings problems to the surface, rather

43
00:02:44.300 --> 00:02:49.100
than hiding them, matters directly for the safety of the people served.

44
00:02:49.250 --> 00:02:54.050
A reflection question from that reading is worth keeping: where would someone

45
00:02:54.200 --> 00:03:00.200
hesitate before reporting an error, and what would it cost the person served if they

46
00:03:00.350 --> 00:03:01.550
stayed silent?

47
00:03:02.300 --> 00:03:05.100
<v Narrator>This is not only a research topic.

48
00:03:05.250 --> 00:03:10.050
One reading reports that staff in the Disability Services Division described limited

49
00:03:10.200 --> 00:03:14.200
psychological safety, performance pressure, and doubt that their input changes

50
00:03:14.350 --> 00:03:15.550
decisions.

51
00:03:15.700 --> 00:03:19.300
The reading calls those practice problems with practice answers.

52
00:03:19.450 --> 00:03:20.650
That framing matters.

53
00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:24.800
It means the answer is not to find braver employees.

54
00:03:24.950 --> 00:03:28.550
It is to change what happens when people speak.

55
00:03:28.700 --> 00:03:30.300
How is disagreement handled?

56
00:03:30.450 --> 00:03:32.050
How are mistakes treated?

57
00:03:32.200 --> 00:03:35.400
Who carries the repair when something lands badly?

58
00:03:35.550 --> 00:03:38.350
And does anyone's input actually change anything?

59
00:03:38.500 --> 00:03:41.300
Those are things other people can see.

60
00:03:41.450 --> 00:03:45.450
They are also things you can practice, whatever your role.

61
00:03:46.200 --> 00:03:48.200
<v Narrator>Here is the path ahead.

62
00:03:48.350 --> 00:03:51.550
First, what psychological safety is and is not.

63
00:03:51.700 --> 00:03:55.700
Then why speaking up costs some people more than others.

64
00:03:55.850 --> 00:04:02.250
Next, how to raise a precise question, and how to receive one about your own work.

65
00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:04.400
After that, mistakes and repair.

66
00:04:04.550 --> 00:04:08.150
Finally, where team habits end and formal routes begin.

67
00:04:08.300 --> 00:04:14.700
Each part closes with a few questions to think over, alone or with a colleague you

68
00:04:14.850 --> 00:04:16.050
trust.

69
00:04:16.200 --> 00:04:20.200
Before you go on, bring one real moment to mind.

70
00:04:20.350 --> 00:04:25.550
A time you held a question back, or a time someone questioned you.

71
00:04:25.700 --> 00:04:28.500
Keep it with you as you go.

